1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,960
Meet Theo.

2
00:00:01,960 --> 00:00:04,197
He's an engineer in an old factory

3
00:00:04,197 --> 00:00:05,193
town in Europe,

4
00:00:05,193 --> 00:00:07,227
and everyone keeps telling him the same

5
00:00:07,227 --> 00:00:08,030
thing.

6
00:00:08,190 --> 00:00:10,360
Europe is falling behind.

7
00:00:10,360 --> 00:00:12,240
America has the tech giants.

8
00:00:12,240 --> 00:00:13,980
China has the factories.

9
00:00:13,980 --> 00:00:15,597
Europe has aging towns,

10
00:00:15,597 --> 00:00:19,010
pricey energy and a lot of nostalgia.

11
00:00:19,240 --> 00:00:21,041
But Theo's town still makes parts for

12
00:00:21,041 --> 00:00:23,044
machines the whole world needs,

13
00:00:23,044 --> 00:00:25,745
and his sister Nora works on medicines

14
00:00:25,745 --> 00:00:27,470
sold around the world.

15
00:00:27,700 --> 00:00:29,692
So here's the question Theo is about

16
00:00:29,692 --> 00:00:30,690
to chase.

17
00:00:30,770 --> 00:00:32,721
What if the technology that seems to

18
00:00:32,721 --> 00:00:34,882
threaten Europe could bring it back?

19
00:00:45,917 --> 00:00:47,721
Start with what Theo sees from his

20
00:00:47,721 --> 00:00:48,837
window.

21
00:00:48,927 --> 00:00:50,887
Europe is not poor.

22
00:00:50,887 --> 00:00:51,852
By the IMF's count,

23
00:00:51,852 --> 00:00:54,215
the European Union's economy is worth

24
00:00:54,215 --> 00:00:56,362
about twenty-one trillion dollars a year,

25
00:00:56,362 --> 00:00:58,497
a little bigger than China's.

26
00:00:58,497 --> 00:00:59,837
It's a giant.

27
00:00:59,837 --> 00:01:02,267
It's just a giant that keeps slipping.

28
00:01:02,297 --> 00:01:04,161
Theo's town has a hospital,

29
00:01:04,161 --> 00:01:06,049
good schools and a train that mostly

30
00:01:06,049 --> 00:01:07,317
runs on time.

31
00:01:07,317 --> 00:01:09,543
That's what a rich place looks like.

32
00:01:09,783 --> 00:01:11,433
Slipping how?

33
00:01:11,513 --> 00:01:13,999
A report for the European Commission,

34
00:01:13,999 --> 00:01:16,297
led by the former central banker Mario

35
00:01:16,297 --> 00:01:16,903
Draghi,

36
00:01:16,903 --> 00:01:19,410
found the gap between Europe and America

37
00:01:19,410 --> 00:01:21,443
has been widening for years.

38
00:01:21,443 --> 00:01:23,203
And most of it comes down to

39
00:01:23,203 --> 00:01:24,683
a single thing.

40
00:01:24,763 --> 00:01:27,463
European workers simply produce less in

41
00:01:27,463 --> 00:01:29,535
the same amount of time.

42
00:01:29,783 --> 00:01:31,734
Theo sees it on his own shop

43
00:01:31,734 --> 00:01:32,613
floor.

44
00:01:32,703 --> 00:01:34,478
The veterans are retiring,

45
00:01:34,478 --> 00:01:36,673
and fewer young people walk in.

46
00:01:36,813 --> 00:01:38,987
Draghi's report warns that Europe will

47
00:01:38,987 --> 00:01:41,403
soon have just two workers for every

48
00:01:41,403 --> 00:01:42,728
retiree.

49
00:01:42,983 --> 00:01:45,173
Then there's the power bill.

50
00:01:45,173 --> 00:01:47,084
European companies pay two to three times

51
00:01:47,084 --> 00:01:48,677
what American companies pay for

52
00:01:48,677 --> 00:01:50,073
electricity.

53
00:01:50,193 --> 00:01:51,935
And when Europe grows a promising tech

54
00:01:51,935 --> 00:01:52,441
startup,

55
00:01:52,441 --> 00:01:54,009
it often packs up and moves to

56
00:01:54,009 --> 00:01:56,003
America to raise money.

57
00:01:56,153 --> 00:01:57,747
Theo's boss has a word for all

58
00:01:57,747 --> 00:01:58,713
of this.

59
00:01:58,713 --> 00:02:00,188
Squeezed.

60
00:02:00,450 --> 00:02:02,570
So that's the paradox.

61
00:02:02,700 --> 00:02:04,242
A rich continent with world-class

62
00:02:04,242 --> 00:02:06,537
universities and famous companies,

63
00:02:06,537 --> 00:02:09,360
and a future everyone is worried about.

64
00:02:09,360 --> 00:02:11,050
Those are the facts.

65
00:02:11,050 --> 00:02:13,188
Now comes Theo's hunch.

66
00:02:17,850 --> 00:02:21,090
The old recipe for growth was simple.

67
00:02:21,100 --> 00:02:24,171
More workers plus more machines makes more

68
00:02:24,171 --> 00:02:25,050
stuff.

69
00:02:25,120 --> 00:02:28,020
Europe's industrial revolution ran on it.

70
00:02:28,060 --> 00:02:29,905
Theo's great-grandfather worked in this

71
00:02:29,905 --> 00:02:31,115
very factory,

72
00:02:31,115 --> 00:02:32,960
shoulder to shoulder with hundreds of

73
00:02:32,960 --> 00:02:33,970
others.

74
00:02:34,120 --> 00:02:34,886
Back then,

75
00:02:34,886 --> 00:02:37,864
Europe was the workshop of the world.

76
00:02:38,117 --> 00:02:40,617
Let me draw Theo's problem.

77
00:02:40,617 --> 00:02:42,863
Output equals the number of workers,

78
00:02:42,863 --> 00:02:45,317
times what each worker produces.

79
00:02:45,377 --> 00:02:47,536
If the number of workers starts falling,

80
00:02:47,536 --> 00:02:49,298
and in Europe it's about to,

81
00:02:49,298 --> 00:02:51,081
there's just a single way to keep

82
00:02:51,081 --> 00:02:52,077
growing.

83
00:02:52,087 --> 00:02:54,999
Each worker has to produce more.

84
00:02:55,250 --> 00:02:58,120
So Theo asks the obvious question.

85
00:02:58,140 --> 00:03:00,606
What if technology let his factory make

86
00:03:00,606 --> 00:03:02,850
far more with far fewer people?

87
00:03:03,130 --> 00:03:05,110
Then a shrinking workforce stops being a

88
00:03:05,110 --> 00:03:06,250
death sentence.

89
00:03:06,250 --> 00:03:08,640
And a different question starts to matter.

90
00:03:08,750 --> 00:03:10,513
Theo writes it on the whiteboard in

91
00:03:10,513 --> 00:03:11,642
the break room.

92
00:03:16,283 --> 00:03:19,083
Theo visits a modern automated plant.

93
00:03:19,083 --> 00:03:21,843
Cameras running AI check every part and

94
00:03:21,843 --> 00:03:23,363
guide robot arms.

95
00:03:23,523 --> 00:03:25,323
The industry's robot federation calls that

96
00:03:25,323 --> 00:03:27,103
kind of machine vision industry-grade.

97
00:03:27,103 --> 00:03:28,613
Sensors warn before a motor fails.

98
00:03:28,813 --> 00:03:31,473
Self-driving carts move parts.

99
00:03:31,473 --> 00:03:32,373
And engineers test the whole line in

100
00:03:32,373 --> 00:03:33,873
a digital twin before building it.

101
00:03:33,923 --> 00:03:38,093
Theo counts the people on the floor.

102
00:03:38,203 --> 00:03:40,459
It doesn't take long.

103
00:03:40,717 --> 00:03:42,967
But the tour guide is honest.

104
00:03:42,967 --> 00:03:45,175
Robots that can learn any job are

105
00:03:45,175 --> 00:03:46,757
not ready yet.

106
00:03:46,897 --> 00:03:48,517
So this isn't a factory with no

107
00:03:48,517 --> 00:03:49,477
people.

108
00:03:49,597 --> 00:03:52,239
It's a factory where each person does

109
00:03:52,239 --> 00:03:53,367
far more.

110
00:03:53,537 --> 00:03:56,033
Theo likes the sound of that.

111
00:03:56,283 --> 00:03:59,173
And Europe isn't starting from scratch.

112
00:03:59,173 --> 00:04:01,406
Germany already runs more robots per

113
00:04:01,406 --> 00:04:03,823
factory worker than America does,

114
00:04:03,823 --> 00:04:06,250
and far more than the world average.

115
00:04:06,517 --> 00:04:08,817
Here's the mechanism that changes Theo's

116
00:04:08,817 --> 00:04:09,857
thinking.

117
00:04:09,857 --> 00:04:12,997
Picture a factory's costs as a stack.

118
00:04:12,997 --> 00:04:15,587
Automation shrinks the labor slice.

119
00:04:15,587 --> 00:04:16,833
But the machines,

120
00:04:16,833 --> 00:04:18,946
the software and the know-how behind them

121
00:04:18,946 --> 00:04:21,447
become a bigger part of the bill.

122
00:04:21,607 --> 00:04:24,297
So the money starts flowing to whoever

123
00:04:24,297 --> 00:04:25,704
owns those things.

124
00:04:25,950 --> 00:04:27,890
Workers don't vanish.

125
00:04:27,890 --> 00:04:29,583
Someone designs the robots,

126
00:04:29,583 --> 00:04:30,633
writes the code,

127
00:04:30,633 --> 00:04:32,090
fixes the line.

128
00:04:32,260 --> 00:04:34,168
But if fewer hands are needed,

129
00:04:34,168 --> 00:04:35,647
cheap labor matters less,

130
00:04:35,647 --> 00:04:38,820
and owning the technology may matter more.

131
00:04:38,970 --> 00:04:40,697
And Europe already owns a lot of

132
00:04:40,697 --> 00:04:41,460
it.

133
00:04:41,460 --> 00:04:44,071
Nora works in the clearest example.

134
00:04:48,717 --> 00:04:49,937
Medicine.

135
00:04:49,937 --> 00:04:51,687
Here's a number that surprises most

136
00:04:51,687 --> 00:04:52,987
Americans.

137
00:04:52,997 --> 00:04:54,478
Last year the EU sold the world

138
00:04:54,478 --> 00:04:56,593
about two hundred and fifty billion

139
00:04:56,593 --> 00:04:59,097
dollars more medicine than it bought.

140
00:04:59,127 --> 00:05:00,617
Its biggest customer?

141
00:05:00,617 --> 00:05:02,335
The United States.

142
00:05:02,583 --> 00:05:05,243
Nora walks Theo past the giants.

143
00:05:05,243 --> 00:05:07,483
Roche and Novartis in Switzerland.

144
00:05:07,483 --> 00:05:10,003
AstraZeneca and GSK in Britain.

145
00:05:10,003 --> 00:05:11,613
Sanofi in France.

146
00:05:11,613 --> 00:05:13,433
Novo Nordisk in Denmark.

147
00:05:13,433 --> 00:05:15,743
Switzerland and Britain are in Europe,

148
00:05:15,743 --> 00:05:17,313
but not in the EU.

149
00:05:17,313 --> 00:05:19,322
Each of them sells medicine all over

150
00:05:19,322 --> 00:05:20,324
the world.

151
00:05:20,583 --> 00:05:22,491
So where does the money in a

152
00:05:22,491 --> 00:05:24,283
medicine come from?

153
00:05:24,283 --> 00:05:25,583
Let me draw it.

154
00:05:25,673 --> 00:05:27,643
Research finds a molecule.

155
00:05:27,643 --> 00:05:29,173
Patents protect it.

156
00:05:29,173 --> 00:05:31,163
Trials prove it works.

157
00:05:31,163 --> 00:05:32,777
Then factories make it,

158
00:05:32,777 --> 00:05:35,483
and trucks and planes ship it worldwide.

159
00:05:35,733 --> 00:05:37,361
Most of the value sits at the

160
00:05:37,361 --> 00:05:38,083
start.

161
00:05:38,083 --> 00:05:39,271
In the ideas,

162
00:05:39,271 --> 00:05:41,327
the patents and the proof.

163
00:05:41,583 --> 00:05:44,445
Nora's favorite example is Novo Nordisk,

164
00:05:44,445 --> 00:05:47,327
the Danish maker of diabetes and obesity

165
00:05:47,327 --> 00:05:48,083
drugs.

166
00:05:48,083 --> 00:05:50,340
It spends almost eight billion dollars a

167
00:05:50,340 --> 00:05:51,713
year on research.

168
00:05:51,713 --> 00:05:53,813
It's expanding its factories too.

169
00:05:53,813 --> 00:05:56,137
But what it really sells is decades

170
00:05:56,137 --> 00:05:58,597
of science that only it controls.

171
00:05:58,850 --> 00:06:01,620
Can AI speed that science up?

172
00:06:01,620 --> 00:06:02,280
Nora is cautious.

173
00:06:02,280 --> 00:06:05,414
AstraZeneca bought a company called

174
00:06:05,414 --> 00:06:08,025
Modella AI to build AI into its

175
00:06:08,025 --> 00:06:09,670
cancer research.

176
00:06:09,830 --> 00:06:11,869
But a new medicine still takes more

177
00:06:11,869 --> 00:06:12,868
than a decade,

178
00:06:12,868 --> 00:06:14,479
and for every success,

179
00:06:14,479 --> 00:06:16,710
thousands of candidates fail.

180
00:06:16,890 --> 00:06:19,130
AI may help pick better bets.

181
00:06:19,130 --> 00:06:22,018
It doesn't guarantee a single approval.

182
00:06:22,283 --> 00:06:25,473
And that's Theo's first big lesson.

183
00:06:25,593 --> 00:06:27,480
You don't have to make everything at

184
00:06:27,480 --> 00:06:29,213
home to capture the value.

185
00:06:29,243 --> 00:06:30,398
You have to own,

186
00:06:30,398 --> 00:06:31,790
and keep improving,

187
00:06:31,790 --> 00:06:34,040
the thing everyone else needs.

188
00:06:38,683 --> 00:06:41,455
Then Nora shows him the most extreme

189
00:06:41,455 --> 00:06:42,363
case.

190
00:06:42,383 --> 00:06:42,796
ASML,

191
00:06:42,796 --> 00:06:44,733
in the Dutch town of Veldhoven,

192
00:06:44,733 --> 00:06:47,127
is the only company that makes the

193
00:06:47,127 --> 00:06:49,303
machines used to print the world's most

194
00:06:49,303 --> 00:06:50,543
advanced chips.

195
00:06:50,543 --> 00:06:53,210
Last year it kept almost eleven billion

196
00:06:53,210 --> 00:06:55,023
dollars in profit.

197
00:06:55,183 --> 00:06:56,273
And it doesn't make a single chip.

198
00:06:56,273 --> 00:06:57,814
Theo has to sit down.

199
00:06:58,050 --> 00:06:59,481
It sits in the middle of a

200
00:06:59,481 --> 00:07:00,170
web.

201
00:07:00,170 --> 00:07:02,926
Thousands of suppliers feed in lenses,

202
00:07:02,926 --> 00:07:04,640
lasers and parts.

203
00:07:04,800 --> 00:07:06,841
Out go machines to chip factories in

204
00:07:06,841 --> 00:07:08,740
Asia and America.

205
00:07:08,990 --> 00:07:11,559
Every time anyone builds a factory for

206
00:07:11,559 --> 00:07:12,179
AI chips,

207
00:07:12,179 --> 00:07:13,678
part of the bill lands in the

208
00:07:13,678 --> 00:07:14,920
Netherlands.

209
00:07:14,980 --> 00:07:16,820
Nora calls it Europe's quietest

210
00:07:16,820 --> 00:07:18,238
superpower.

211
00:07:18,483 --> 00:07:21,173
Theo recognizes the next example.

212
00:07:21,173 --> 00:07:21,850
Siemens,

213
00:07:21,850 --> 00:07:22,760
from Munich,

214
00:07:22,760 --> 00:07:24,836
sells factories their controllers,

215
00:07:24,836 --> 00:07:26,680
automation and software,

216
00:07:26,680 --> 00:07:28,913
including digital twins.

217
00:07:29,203 --> 00:07:31,636
Its chief executive calls industrial AI an

218
00:07:31,636 --> 00:07:34,263
exceptional growth opportunity.

219
00:07:34,493 --> 00:07:35,962
When the world automates,

220
00:07:35,962 --> 00:07:38,422
somebody sells the automation.

221
00:07:38,683 --> 00:07:40,559
And then there's Airbus,

222
00:07:40,559 --> 00:07:43,063
run from near Toulouse in France.

223
00:07:43,133 --> 00:07:44,493
Its real product is coordination.

224
00:07:44,493 --> 00:07:46,980
Thousands of parts from many countries,

225
00:07:46,980 --> 00:07:50,471
engineered and certified to fit together,

226
00:07:50,471 --> 00:07:53,583
with years of orders already waiting.

227
00:07:53,903 --> 00:07:56,195
That's exactly where smarter software and

228
00:07:56,195 --> 00:07:58,399
automation could pay off.

229
00:07:58,650 --> 00:08:00,880
Theo stays skeptical.

230
00:08:00,930 --> 00:08:03,380
Europe doesn't dominate everything.

231
00:08:03,420 --> 00:08:05,517
China installs far more robots,

232
00:08:05,517 --> 00:08:07,638
and most cloud and consumer tech is

233
00:08:07,638 --> 00:08:09,180
American.

234
00:08:09,370 --> 00:08:11,897
But Europe owns capabilities that could

235
00:08:11,897 --> 00:08:13,734
gain value as production gets more

236
00:08:13,734 --> 00:08:15,253
automated.

237
00:08:19,917 --> 00:08:22,677
Which brings Theo to the big idea.

238
00:08:22,677 --> 00:08:23,818
For decades,

239
00:08:23,818 --> 00:08:26,379
towns like his competed to host factories

240
00:08:26,379 --> 00:08:27,657
and their jobs.

241
00:08:27,657 --> 00:08:29,497
In a more automated world,

242
00:08:29,497 --> 00:08:32,577
the factory floor may employ fewer people.

243
00:08:32,657 --> 00:08:35,021
And the question shifts from who operates

244
00:08:35,021 --> 00:08:37,340
the machines to who owns them.

245
00:08:37,583 --> 00:08:40,833
So Theo sketches his own company's future.

246
00:08:40,883 --> 00:08:42,613
Call it Company E.

247
00:08:42,623 --> 00:08:44,258
Its engineers in Europe develop the

248
00:08:44,258 --> 00:08:45,443
technology.

249
00:08:45,443 --> 00:08:47,605
It owns the patents and the software,

250
00:08:47,605 --> 00:08:50,006
makes the key decisions and carries the

251
00:08:50,006 --> 00:08:51,043
big risks.

252
00:08:51,073 --> 00:08:53,255
Partner factories in other countries make

253
00:08:53,255 --> 00:08:55,243
the product under license.

254
00:08:55,483 --> 00:08:57,212
Now watch the money,

255
00:08:57,212 --> 00:08:59,863
with made-up numbers on screen.

256
00:08:59,943 --> 00:09:02,133
The partner factory earns a small,

257
00:09:02,133 --> 00:09:04,503
steady fee on top of its costs.

258
00:09:04,523 --> 00:09:06,823
Company E keeps what's left.

259
00:09:06,823 --> 00:09:09,359
Then the factory automates,

260
00:09:09,359 --> 00:09:10,983
and its costs fall.

261
00:09:11,113 --> 00:09:12,736
Its fee shrinks a little,

262
00:09:12,736 --> 00:09:15,453
because it's charged on lower costs.

263
00:09:15,463 --> 00:09:17,925
And Company E's share of each sale

264
00:09:17,925 --> 00:09:19,755
grows by more than half.

265
00:09:20,017 --> 00:09:22,477
But there are catches.

266
00:09:22,657 --> 00:09:24,998
This only works if Company E really

267
00:09:24,998 --> 00:09:25,828
does the work,

268
00:09:25,828 --> 00:09:27,084
and if prices hold,

269
00:09:27,084 --> 00:09:29,387
while rivals automate too.

270
00:09:29,457 --> 00:09:31,949
And simply registering a patent somewhere

271
00:09:31,949 --> 00:09:33,487
doesn't move the profit.

272
00:09:33,487 --> 00:09:34,364
The real work,

273
00:09:34,364 --> 00:09:36,613
the people and the decisions have to

274
00:09:36,613 --> 00:09:37,662
be there.

275
00:09:37,917 --> 00:09:40,285
So the question for Europe isn't whether

276
00:09:40,285 --> 00:09:42,977
it can win back the assembly line.

277
00:09:42,977 --> 00:09:45,130
It's whether it can attract and keep

278
00:09:45,130 --> 00:09:46,302
the companies,

279
00:09:46,302 --> 00:09:47,235
the scientists,

280
00:09:47,235 --> 00:09:49,627
the money and the ideas that profit

281
00:09:49,627 --> 00:09:52,737
from automated production everywhere.

282
00:09:52,737 --> 00:09:55,607
Theo circles that sentence on his sketch.

283
00:09:55,850 --> 00:09:57,640
Then Theo's boss throws in a twist.

284
00:09:57,640 --> 00:09:59,632
If robots make labor matter less,

285
00:09:59,632 --> 00:10:01,180
other costs matter more.

286
00:10:01,180 --> 00:10:02,960
Energy.

287
00:10:02,960 --> 00:10:03,690
Money.

288
00:10:03,690 --> 00:10:04,350
Logistics.

289
00:10:04,350 --> 00:10:06,290
Stability.

290
00:10:06,290 --> 00:10:08,640
And the taxes on producing.

291
00:10:08,750 --> 00:10:09,278
Brazil,

292
00:10:09,278 --> 00:10:10,170
for example,

293
00:10:10,170 --> 00:10:11,992
gets most of its electricity from

294
00:10:11,992 --> 00:10:13,320
renewables.

295
00:10:13,410 --> 00:10:15,770
Yet an industry study found that almost

296
00:10:15,770 --> 00:10:17,916
half of what its power companies bill

297
00:10:17,916 --> 00:10:19,611
goes to taxes and charges,

298
00:10:19,611 --> 00:10:21,972
and the country is now overhauling its

299
00:10:21,972 --> 00:10:23,430
sales taxes.

300
00:10:23,580 --> 00:10:25,636
So a country with cheap power would

301
00:10:25,636 --> 00:10:28,520
still need competitive costs to produce.

302
00:10:28,530 --> 00:10:30,887
And even if Company E's partner plant

303
00:10:30,887 --> 00:10:32,151
ends up in Brazil,

304
00:10:32,151 --> 00:10:34,508
the profit from the technology could still

305
00:10:34,508 --> 00:10:36,179
come home to Europe.

306
00:10:40,817 --> 00:10:43,176
Can Europe pull in the people and

307
00:10:43,176 --> 00:10:46,377
the money that kind of company needs?

308
00:10:46,377 --> 00:10:48,367
It has real pull.

309
00:10:48,367 --> 00:10:49,150
Strong courts,

310
00:10:49,150 --> 00:10:50,203
research labs,

311
00:10:50,203 --> 00:10:52,067
a huge single market,

312
00:10:52,067 --> 00:10:53,660
industrial clusters,

313
00:10:53,660 --> 00:10:56,691
and cities people want to live in.

314
00:10:56,950 --> 00:10:59,640
But Nora warns him.

315
00:10:59,650 --> 00:11:01,650
Wealthy residents are not the same as

316
00:11:01,650 --> 00:11:03,450
productive investment.

317
00:11:03,450 --> 00:11:05,763
A head office is not a research

318
00:11:05,763 --> 00:11:06,620
lab.

319
00:11:06,660 --> 00:11:10,414
Only real activity builds real growth.

320
00:11:10,650 --> 00:11:13,070
And newcomers bring problems.

321
00:11:13,120 --> 00:11:14,420
Pricier housing.

322
00:11:14,420 --> 00:11:15,950
Wider inequality.

323
00:11:15,950 --> 00:11:18,170
Shortages of skilled workers.

324
00:11:18,170 --> 00:11:20,729
Rich arrivals don't automatically pay for

325
00:11:20,729 --> 00:11:22,510
pensions and hospitals.

326
00:11:22,580 --> 00:11:24,433
That depends on what they do and

327
00:11:24,433 --> 00:11:25,960
how they're taxed.

328
00:11:25,980 --> 00:11:28,090
Nora has watched the rents climb in

329
00:11:28,090 --> 00:11:29,863
her own neighborhood.

330
00:11:34,517 --> 00:11:36,991
So what would a comeback take,

331
00:11:36,991 --> 00:11:39,537
for Theo's town and for Europe?

332
00:11:39,587 --> 00:11:40,847
Let me list it.

333
00:11:40,847 --> 00:11:43,677
Let companies grow big without leaving.

334
00:11:43,677 --> 00:11:45,917
Make money easier to raise.

335
00:11:45,917 --> 00:11:47,837
Bring down energy costs.

336
00:11:47,837 --> 00:11:50,662
Actually use AI in everyday factories and

337
00:11:50,662 --> 00:11:51,837
offices.

338
00:11:51,837 --> 00:11:54,107
And compete hard for talent.

339
00:11:54,107 --> 00:11:54,758
Miss these,

340
00:11:54,758 --> 00:11:57,153
and the profits flow elsewhere.

341
00:11:57,417 --> 00:11:57,992
So,

342
00:11:57,992 --> 00:12:01,317
could AI bring Europe back to power?

343
00:12:01,487 --> 00:12:03,927
Not by building the most factories.

344
00:12:03,927 --> 00:12:05,827
That race is China's.

345
00:12:05,957 --> 00:12:08,657
And not by copying Silicon Valley.

346
00:12:08,927 --> 00:12:10,195
But as the owner,

347
00:12:10,195 --> 00:12:11,248
developer,

348
00:12:11,248 --> 00:12:14,042
financier and licensor of technology the

349
00:12:14,042 --> 00:12:15,717
whole world runs on.

350
00:12:15,877 --> 00:12:17,422
It's a plausible path,

351
00:12:17,422 --> 00:12:18,917
not a promise.

352
00:12:19,077 --> 00:12:20,957
Europe has the assets.

353
00:12:20,957 --> 00:12:23,321
The rest depends on choices it hasn't

354
00:12:23,321 --> 00:12:24,446
made yet.

355
00:12:24,683 --> 00:12:25,891
For generations,

356
00:12:25,891 --> 00:12:28,143
economic power meant factories and

357
00:12:28,143 --> 00:12:29,123
workers.

358
00:12:29,123 --> 00:12:30,726
In a world run more and more

359
00:12:30,726 --> 00:12:31,848
by machines,

360
00:12:31,848 --> 00:12:33,573
the question may change.

361
00:12:33,573 --> 00:12:35,393
Not just who makes the products,

362
00:12:35,393 --> 00:12:37,213
but who owns the technology,

363
00:12:37,213 --> 00:12:40,063
the machines and the ideas behind them.

364
00:12:40,283 --> 00:12:42,217
And for Theo's old factory town,

365
00:12:42,217 --> 00:12:44,389
that could be a very different way

366
00:12:44,389 --> 00:12:46,075
back into the race.

367
00:12:46,317 --> 00:12:46,914
Next time,

368
00:12:46,914 --> 00:12:49,590
we follow the money behind another hidden

369
00:12:49,590 --> 00:12:51,787
link of the world economy.

370
00:12:51,787 --> 00:12:53,894
This is The Hidden Economics.
